C.append({'k': 'labelled_block', 'label': 'Abstract', 'abstract': True, 'text':
"This study develops and validates FreshCheck, an image-based classifier that labels food as fresh or spoiled and refers uncertain images to staff instead of guessing. FreshCheck averages the class probabilities of three logistic-regression models built on colour and texture features and on frozen MobileNetV3 and ResNet-50 features, and refers an image when its highest probability falls below a threshold set on training data so that at most 1% of spoiled images are accepted as fresh. It was evaluated on public red-meat (2,266 images), chicken-breast (1,300) and fruit (1,894) datasets with capture-session-grouped cross-validation, after every dataset had been screened for duplicate and near-duplicate images. Screening showed that consecutive red-meat files were frames of one session, that fruit photographs recurred, and that 491 of 500 'good' files in a public paneer-curry set were copies of nine photographs; image-level splits therefore overstated accuracy (red meat: 98.4% versus 86.9% with sessions held out; p = 0.048). Under session-grouped validation FreshCheck reached 90.3% on red meat, 97.2% on chicken and 97.2% on fruit, above every single-feature baseline. On chicken the refer rule decided 91.9% of images with 99.0% accuracy and lowered the share of spoiled samples accepted as fresh from 3.2% to 1.0%. Models moved to the other meat dataset without local images failed (50–55%); 31–70 labelled local images restored 80–91%, and the other dataset added nothing beyond those local images. A demonstration application and all code are released. Image-based freshness checks should be validated by session and calibrated with local images before use."})
C.append({'k': 'labelled_block', 'label': 'Keywords', 'text': 'Food freshness; Spoilage detection; Computer vision; Selective classification; Model validation; Food safety'})

# ---------------- 1 Introduction ----------------
C.append(H1('1. Introduction'))
C.append(P("This study develops an image-based classifier that sorts food into fresh and spoiled, and tests it in the way it would be used at a new site. Visual assessment of freshness is a routine control point at receiving, in storage and at service for meat, fresh produce and prepared dishes, and camera-based checks promise to make it faster and more consistent (Meenu et al., 2021; Zhou et al., 2019). Studies built on public meat image datasets report 98.5–99.8% accuracy for fresh, half-fresh and spoiled categories (Abd Elfattah et al., 2025; Büyükarıkan, 2024; Külcü & Balpetek Külcü, 2026), and frozen-network features have been compared across several public meat collections (Hidalgo et al., 2025)."))
C.append(P("Three things are still missing before such results can support a real control. The first is sound validation. A test score means something only if the test images are independent of the training images in the ways that matter in use, and food analytics has begun to show what happens when they are not: an electronic-nose model of beef freshness reached a macro-F1 of 0.97 on a random split but 0.50 when whole cuts were held out (Ozkat, 2026), and specimen-disjoint validation raised the error of avocado shelf-life models from 2.37 to 3.12 days (Mudau et al., 2026). Public food image datasets are exposed to the same problem, because they often contain many photographs of a few items taken in a few sessions and seldom record item or session identifiers. One recent study removed 43.95% of the frames of merged meat corpora by perceptual hashing and left group-wise splitting for future work (Bramantyo et al., 2026), but whether hashing is enough has not been tested."))
C.append(P("The second gap is how errors are handled. A freshness check fails most seriously when it accepts spoiled food as fresh, yet published classifiers always return a label, however uncertain, and report overall accuracy rather than this error. Letting a classifier abstain when its confidence is low is an established way of trading coverage for reliability (Chow, 1970), and in a kitchen or receiving bay an abstention has a natural meaning: the item is passed to a trained person. The third gap is deployment. A model trained on one public dataset will meet food, cameras and lighting it has not seen, and how many local images are needed to make it work there has not been measured."))
C.append(P("Our aim was to develop and validate FreshCheck, a leakage-aware image classifier for fresh and spoiled food with a refer-to-staff option. The specific objectives were to (i) screen three public datasets (red meat, chicken breast and fruit) for duplicate, near-duplicate and capture-session structure, and four prepared-food collections for exact copies, so that validation folds could respect that structure; (ii) quantify how far image-level validation overstates accuracy compared with capture-session-grouped validation; (iii) build FreshCheck by late fusion of colour and texture features and two frozen deep feature sets, and compare it with single-feature baselines, early fusion and a fine-tuned network under session-grouped validation; (iv) evaluate the refer rule by its effect on spoiled samples accepted as fresh; and (v) measure performance on an independently collected dataset and how many local labelled images restore it. A demonstration application and all code are released for reuse."))

