C.append(H2('2.2. Dataset screening for duplicates and capture sessions'))
C.append(P("Before any model was trained, each dataset was screened for structure that would let related images fall on both sides of a split. For every image we computed a 256-bit perceptual hash (pHash on a 16 × 16 discrete cosine transform; Buchner, n.d.) and the Hamming distance between all pairs; images within 60 bits of each other were linked, and the connected components became near-duplicate families. For scale, the 0.1st percentile of all pairwise distances was 100 bits in the red-meat set and 102 bits in the poultry set. Each file name carries a class prefix and a running number (for example FRESH-1000 or segar_0001), and we tested whether that number tracks capture order in two ways: by recording whether each image's closest neighbour by hash was a same-class image within five file numbers, against a chance rate from random same-class pairs, and by averaging the hash distance between same-class images as a function of the gap between their file numbers. For the paneer-curry and momo sets, exact copies were identified by file checksums."))
C.append(P("We turned the first test into a provenance check that can be run before training. Images are ranked by file number within each class, and the statistic is the share whose closest same-class neighbour by hash lies within five places in that ranking. Its null distribution comes from 999 random within-class permutations of the ranks, so p = (1 + number of permutations at or above the observed share) / 1,000. Alongside it we report the share of images with a same-class neighbour within 30 bits, a stricter threshold that counts only near-copies. The check needs only file names and image hashes. Its result decides how validation folds are grouped (Section 2.5): by capture-order block when file order encodes sessions, and by near-copy family when copies are scattered."))

C.append(H2('2.3. The FreshCheck classifier'))
C.append({'k': 'fig', 'file': 'fig1_pipeline_v2.png', 'caption': "Figure 1. How FreshCheck reaches a decision, shown for one half-fresh red-meat photograph with models trained without its capture session. The photograph is described in three ways (a hue histogram and texture map, and the outputs of two pretrained networks; only part of each network output is shown), each description feeds its own logistic regression, and the three probability vectors are averaged. Here the ResNet-50 model alone would have called the meat fresh; the average calls it half-fresh. If the top averaged probability is below the threshold τ, the photograph is referred to staff. τ is chosen on training photographs only, as the smallest value at which no more than 1% of spoiled photographs are accepted as fresh."})
C.append(P("FreshCheck (Fig. 1) combines three feature sets that describe a photograph in different ways. The colour and texture set (50 values) combines marginal HSV histograms (18, 8 and 8 bins), a 10-bin histogram of uniform local binary patterns (8 neighbours, radius 1) and the mean and standard deviation of each Lab channel, all computed at 224 × 224 pixels, and so captures discolouration and surface texture directly. The two deep sets are the 1,024-dimensional pooled output of MobileNetV3-Small (Howard et al., 2019) and the 2,048-dimensional pooled output of ResNet-50 (He et al., 2016; timm weights resnet50.a1_in1k), both pretrained on ImageNet and used frozen with their default preprocessing (Wightman, 2019). A small and a larger network were chosen so that the system runs on an ordinary computer without a graphics card. Each feature set feeds its own standardised, L2-regularised logistic regression (C = 1), and FreshCheck averages the three predicted class-probability vectors (late fusion). Colour features were not computed for the fruit set, where FreshCheck fused the two deep sets."))
C.append(P("If the largest averaged probability is below a threshold τ, FreshCheck returns 'Refer to staff' instead of a class. τ is chosen on the training data alone. Within each training set, a grouped three-fold cross-validation, with the same grouping as the outer folds, produces out-of-fold probabilities, and τ is the smallest value between 0.50 and 0.99 (steps of 0.01) at which no more than 1% of the spoiled training images are accepted as fresh. The threshold thus encodes a safety target set by the user rather than a value tuned on test data. For the released models, trained on all images, τ is the median of the values chosen in cross-validation (0.50 for red meat and 0.74 for chicken)."))

C.append(H2('2.4. Baseline models'))
C.append(P("FreshCheck was compared with each feature set on its own, with the same logistic regression, and with early fusion, in which the three feature sets were concatenated and fed to a single logistic regression. To check that freezing the networks does not explain the results, MobileNetV3-Small was also fine-tuned end to end on the red-meat set: 160 × 160 inputs, four epochs, AdamW (learning rate 5 × 10⁻⁴, weight decay 10⁻⁴, cosine schedule), batch size 32, random-resized-crop (70–100% of the area) and horizontal-flip augmentation, and a centre crop at test time. Training ran on a two-core CPU, so fine-tuning used three fold assignments (seeds 0–2) for image-level folds and capture-order blocks of 100, and one for random groups of 100."))

C.append(H2('2.5. Validation protocols'))
C.append(P("The main protocol was five-fold cross-validation with capture sessions kept together, built with stratified group k-fold and repeated with five fold assignments (seeds 0–4). Following the screening results, red-meat and chicken images were grouped in blocks of 100 consecutive file numbers within each class (capture-order blocks), and fruit images in near-copy families (images within 30 bits; at 60 bits, fruit families also joined different fruit photographed on similar plain backgrounds). Image-level stratified five-fold cross-validation was run for comparison."))
C.append(P("To show how much the validation design matters, the single-feature models were also evaluated on the official splits, with hash families kept together, and with capture-order blocks of 5, 10, 20, 50 and 100 images. Grouping on its own makes folds coarser, so random within-class groups of 5, 20 and 100 images served as a size-matched control. For block sizes of 20 and 100 we drew 20 random within-class groupings, each with its own fold assignment, as a null distribution; the empirical p-value is one plus the number of random groupings scoring at or below the best capture-order run, divided by 21, so 0.048 is the smallest value attainable. In the fruit set the null distribution came from 20 random regroupings that kept the family size distribution."))

C.append(H2('2.6. Transfer to a new dataset and local labelling'))
C.append(P("To mimic installation at a new site, the red-meat and chicken datasets were reduced to fresh versus spoiled by leaving out the half-fresh red-meat class (1,477 red-meat images remain). Models were trained on every image of one dataset, with τ chosen by grouped cross-validation on that dataset, and tested on every image of the other, in both directions. The single-feature and fine-tuned models were tested in the same way. We then added k = 1, 2, 5 or 10 capture sessions per class from the new dataset (blocks of 20 consecutive file numbers) to the training data and tested on all remaining sessions of the new dataset, with 20 random draws of sessions for each k. As a control, the same local images were used alone, without the other dataset."))

C.append(H2('2.7. Demonstration application'))
C.append(P("A demonstration application was written to show how FreshCheck would be used. FreshCheck models were trained on all images of the red-meat and chicken datasets and saved with their thresholds. The application offers a web interface built with Gradio and a command-line mode: the user chooses the food type and uploads one or more photographs, and the application returns the class probabilities and the decision, either a class or 'Refer to staff'. It states that it is a research prototype and that 'Fresh' means only that no visible problem was found. Processing time was measured on 20 test images on a two-core CPU without a graphics card."))

C.append(H2('2.8. Performance metrics and statistical analysis'))
C.append(P("We report accuracy, macro-F1 and the spoiled-as-fresh rate, the share of all spoiled test images that were returned as fresh; for three-class tasks a spoiled image returned as half-fresh or normal is not counted. For the refer rule we also report coverage (the share of images given a class), accuracy on the images given a class, and the shares of fresh and spoiled images referred. Cross-validated metrics are pooled over all held-out predictions and given as mean ± standard deviation across fold assignments. The analysis used Python 3.13 with scikit-learn 1.9, PyTorch 2.15, timm 1.0, OpenCV 5.0, scikit-image 0.26, ImageHash 4.3 and Gradio 6.32. The scripts that reproduce every number in this paper, the per-image hash families and fold groupings, the trained models and the application are supplied as Supplementary material."))

