C.append(P("Screening found strong session structure in the red-meat set. Of its images, 53.7% had as their closest neighbour by hash a same-class image within five file numbers, against 1.2% expected by chance, and same-class images one file number apart differed by 103.9 bits on average, compared with 127.4 bits beyond a gap of 400 (Fig. 3d). Six consecutive fresh files are frames of the same cut, 10–48 bits apart (Fig. 2a), whereas six fresh files drawn at random show different cuts (Fig. 2b). Of the 451 official test images, 87 (19%) belonged to a hash family that also had members in the training set. The chicken set showed none of this: consecutive files show different pieces, 120–136 bits apart (Fig. 2e), the nearest-neighbour share was 0.6% against 1.3% by chance, and every image formed its own hash family. In the fruit set, whose file numbers are interleaved across classes, 68.2% of images had a same-class near-copy within 30 bits elsewhere in the set, and the largest family held 36 images, such as the same two rotten bananas photographed about every eleventh file (Fig. 2c)."))
C.append(P("The provenance check summarised these patterns (Table 2). It flagged the red-meat set (56.1% of nearest neighbours within five places against a permutation mean of 1.3%; p = 0.001) and the fruit set (22.4% against 1.6%; p = 0.001), and cleared the chicken set (1.9% against 1.5%; p = 0.19). The near-copy share told the two flagged sets apart: 5.3% in red meat, where frames of one session differ by more than a hash detector allows, and 68.2% in fruit, where copies are close enough for hashing to find. Ranks rather than raw file numbers are used in the check, which is why the red-meat figure (56.1%) differs slightly from the 53.7% above."))
