C.append(H2('3.2. Effect of validation design'))
C.append(P("On the red-meat set, frozen MobileNetV3 features gave 98.2% on the official split and 98.4% with image-level folds (Table 3), close to the 99.78% and 99.66% published for the same dataset under the same protocols (Külcü & Balpetek Külcü, 2026). Keeping hash families together barely mattered (98.1%), and random groups of 20 and 100 images gave 98.5%. Capture-order blocks did matter: accuracy fell steadily as blocks grew, to 95.8%, 94.5%, 92.4%, 90.7% and 86.9% for blocks of 5, 10, 20, 50 and 100 images (Fig. 3a), below every one of 20 random groupings of the same size (p = 0.048; Table 4). Most of the extra errors (85% at blocks of 20) were confusions between fresh and half-fresh. ResNet-50 features (98.2% to 87.4%), colour and texture features (89.8% to 83.5%; Fig. 3b) and the fine-tuned network (97.7% ± 0.3% to 85.7% ± 1.7%) followed the same pattern, so fine-tuning raised the apparent ceiling without closing the gap between seen and unseen sessions."))
C.append(P("In the chicken set, which the provenance check cleared, the same protocols moved accuracy by 1.0–2.7 points (Fig. 3c; Table 4), and ResNet-50 features did not differ from random grouping (p = 0.14 and 0.86). In the fruit set, keeping near-copy families together lowered three-class accuracy from 97.8% to 96.6% (p = 0.048 against 20 random regroupings), so errors rose by about half, while the easier fresh-versus-rotten task did not change (99.5% under both protocols). Image-level validation therefore overstates accuracy where the screening finds structure, and session-grouped validation was used for every comparison that follows."))
